  "notes": "West leads the ♦6 against 6♠. West's diamond lead (an obvious singleton) is won by South. A top spade reveals the 3-0 trump break and marks West with 3712 distribution. It looks like South will need some sort of trump squeeze against East.\n\nSouth should continue with a low trump to the dummy. The ♥A is cashed for a club discard. A heart is ruffed high. South crosses again in spades, drawing West's last trump in the process. On dummy's last heart East is forced to discard a club while South ruffs.\n\nSouth now ducks a club. If East wins, South will be able to establish a club trick by ruffing. If East plays low, allowing West to win the club trick, West's only hope is to play a heart.\n\nSouth can take the ruff in either hand and subsequently squeeze East. If South ruffs in the dummy, for example, he can next cross to his hand in diamonds. On the play of the last trump, East is squeezed in diamonds and clubs.",
  "commentary_paragraphs": [
    "West leads the ♦6 against 6♠. West's diamond lead (an obvious singleton) is won by South. A top spade reveals the 3-0 trump break and marks West with 3712 distribution. It looks like South will need some sort of trump squeeze against East.",
